{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the woman said, etc.</b> She found a clever way to respond to everything heard from his words, according to what we explained, for he intended three claims, and she answered him, corresponding to what was heard in his words: (1) that everything is forbidden — or according to the claim that all the trees of the garden are branches of that blessed tree and still draw sustenance from the Tree of Knowledge — or according to the claim that he is the witness who heard that the Creator forbade everything. She said: Not so; from every tree of the garden we may eat, for HaShem, who forbade the Tree of Knowledge, is the one who permitted the other trees — so what if they are blessed from it? Granted, if HaShem had not explicitly permitted all the trees of the garden, there would be room to entertain your words. But since HaShem explicitly permitted all the trees of the garden, what place is there for us to forbid something permitted by the mouth of the Creator? Also, there is no need to heed his testimony, for his testimony is contradicted in her eyes by the words of her husband, and the contradiction of testimonies serves to prevent forbidding the trees of the garden, since it is disputed testimony — but it does not serve to contradict all the testimony to permit everything, for included in everything the serpent says is [the prohibition of] the Tree of Knowledge, and according to the opinion of Beit Hillel." "<b>And corresponding to</b> what was heard from his words in the extravagant praise of the tree, she said: On the contrary — because of its lowliness the Creator forbade it, as it says <b>lest you die</b>, for it has an aspect of death in it, and Elohim said to us: you shall not eat, etc., lest you die. And for you to understand the intended meaning, you must know that everything YHWH commands His people — YHWH did not command anything out of anger and vengeance, but everything is for the purpose of distancing harm and drawing near benefit. And you see how many plantings YHWH planted, and how many wondrous matters He created in the world, and all were given to the work of His hands. And these are the words of Chavah, who reasoned that HaShem did not withhold the Tree of Knowledge from her except because it is the harmful portion, and she reasoned well. And thus she said: מִכָּל עֵץ הַגָּן נֹאכֵל — meaning, this declares that HaShem desires to bestow good out of His will, and from this you learn that what He withheld from us from this is for the purpose of distancing harm. And she added to say <b>and you shall not touch it</b>, even though it was not said in the words of HaShem. She reasoned: since the withholding is for the purpose that you not come to harm, from now on whatever you add to distance from the aspect of harm is proper to do. And it is possible that the man, when he commanded her concerning the tree in this manner, said it to her — to increase the distancing — when you know that this tree is a deadly poison, you will go to the extreme in distancing yourself." "<b>Further,</b> she intended to say to him: Let it be that just as your words say, so it is — for it is the most praised of all the trees of the garden. But what of it if the tree is more praiseworthy than all of them? Behold, Elohim said that if we eat of it we incur death, and we must be concerned for ourselves. And her saying ״וְלֹא תִגְּעוּ בּוֹ״ — it is possible that she was concerned about the oil of its fruits, and through touching [it] the body would benefit, as they said (Shabbat 86a): From where do we derive that anointing is like… etc., and behold, she would derive benefit from it, and it is included in eating, as they said in the Talmud (Pesachim 21b): Everywhere it says לֹא תֹאכְלוּ, etc. — one [prohibition] is the prohibition of eating, etc. And the matter is precise: for even though it has an exalted virtue as his words [suggest], and nothing compares to it — still its feet go down to death; in this it is lower than all the trees of the garden." "<b>And from now on</b> there remains no room to argue further ״Arise, eat.״ And in any event (מִמָּה נַפְשָׁךְ), this withholding that HaShem withheld from us from this tree will not be withheld from its portion: either for the purpose of distancing from harm, or for the purpose of accepting upon ourselves the decree of the King and heeding His voice — for this is among the courtesies of the kingdom. And for both reasons there is fear of death in the matter, and upon us is it to distance ourselves."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:24.621896730Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}